---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro';
import CodeBlock from '~/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import { VERSIONS } from '~/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="libleptris — schema validation docs (XSD, RelaxNG, Schematron, DTD)"
  description="Four schema families in one engine: XSD, RELAX NG, Schematron and DTD validation — compile once, validate many, from C, the CLI, Ruby and Python, with a capability probe for each face."
>
  <DocsShell
    crumb="Schema validation"
    title={`Schema validation — XSD, RelaxNG, Schematron, DTD (engine v${VERSIONS.lib})`}
    lede="libleptris validates documents against four schema families: W3C XML Schema (XSD), RELAX NG, Schematron and DTD. Every family follows the same shape — compile the schema once into an immutable handle, validate any number of documents against it, and collect every violation rather than stopping at the first."
  >
    <h2 id="model">The model</h2>
    <p>
      Each family compiles a schema document (or a set of them) into a
      handle, validates parsed documents against it, and reports
      failures in the family&rsquo;s native diagnostic style — the
      engine speaks each vocabulary instead of flattening them into one
      generic shape:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="text"
      code={`XSD         element 'server' @1:22: attribute 'port': lexical value does not match its type
RELAX NG    1:10: error: element "server" missing required attribute "port"   (Jing's spelling)
Schematron  the rule's <assert> fired (boolean result, or the SVRL document)
DTD         a structured error record (element name, position, message)`}
    />
    <p>
      Whether a build carries a face is answerable at runtime — the
      capability probe returns a bitmask:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`LeptrisCapabilities caps = leptris_capabilities();
caps.caps & LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD;         // XSD compile + validate
caps.caps & LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD_SET;     // multi-document schema sets
caps.caps & LEPTRIS_CAP_RELAXNG;     // RELAX NG
caps.caps & LEPTRIS_CAP_SCHEMATRON;  // Schematron
caps.caps & LEPTRIS_CAP_DTD;         // DTD`}
    />

    <h2 id="cli">The CLI</h2>
    <p>
      <code>leptris validate</code> takes <code>--xsd</code>,
      <code>--rng</code>, <code>--schematron</code> or
      <code>--dtd</code>, prints every finding, and exits non-zero when
      anything fails:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="console"
      code={`$ leptris validate --xsd port.xsd bad.xml
xsd: invalid
  element 'server' @1:10: attribute 'port': required but absent
$ echo $?   # 1

$ leptris validate --rng server.rng bad.xml
rng: invalid
  1:10: error: element "server" missing required attribute "port"`}
    />

    <h2 id="xsd">XSD — W3C XML Schema</h2>
    <p>
      The C face lives in <code>&lt;leptris.h&gt;</code>: compile from
      text, a file, or a multi-document set; validate; enumerate every
      failure:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <string.h>
#include <leptris.h>
#include <leptris/dom/document.h>

const char* xsd =
    "<xs:schema xmlns:xs='http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema'>"
    " <xs:element name='server'><xs:complexType>"
    "  <xs:attribute name='port' type='xs:integer' use='required'/>"
    " </xs:complexType></xs:element></xs:schema>";

LeptrisStatus st = LEPTRIS_OK;
LeptrisXsdSchema s = leptris_xsd_compile(xsd, strlen(xsd), &st);
if (st != LEPTRIS_OK) { fprintf(stderr, "%s\\n", leptris_xsd_error(s)); return 1; }

LeptrisDocument d =
    leptris_parse_string("<server port=\\"http\\"/>", 21, &st);
if (leptris_xsd_validate(s, d) == 0)
    for (size_t i = 0; i < leptris_xsd_error_count(s); i++)
        printf("%s\\n", leptris_xsd_error_at(s, i));
// => element 'server' @1:22: attribute 'port': lexical value does not match its type

leptris_document_free(d);
leptris_xsd_free(s);`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>leptris_xsd_compile_set(paths, n, &amp;st)</code> compiles
      several schema documents into one schema — declarations merge
      across the whole set, with a shared visited-set closing
      include/import cycles between members, so components declared in
      importing documents are visible to the head. Validation is
      position-aware: each finding names the element, its line and
      column, and the failed component. The remaining C faces:
      <code>leptris_xsd_declaration_count</code> enumerates top-level
      declarations; <code>leptris_xsd_builtin_valid</code>,
      <code>leptris_xsd_simple_valid</code> and
      <code>leptris_xsd_content_valid</code> expose the datatype,
      facet and content-model layers directly;
      <code>leptris_xsd_version</code> reports the effective XSD
      version (<code>"1.0"</code> or <code>"1.1"</code>); and
      <code>leptris_xsd_error_report</code> hands back the whole
      finding list as structured records (failure class, component,
      line, column, message).
    </p>
    <Callout kind="note">
      Status, stated plainly (issue <code>#1075</code>, closed): the
      tier-1 charter ships — compilation (include/import/redefine,
      derivation, groups, substitution groups), the built-in datatype
      table with facets, content models, instance validation with
      accumulated findings, and <code>xs:key</code>/
      <code>xs:unique</code>/<code>xs:keyref</code> identity
      constraints — and so does the XSD 1.1 set: <code>xs:assert</code>,
      <code>xs:assertion</code>, <code>xs:alternative</code>
      (conditional type assignment) and <code>xs:openContent</code>.
      A schema opts into 1.1 semantics through the root
      <code>@version="1.1"</code> attribute or the versioning
      namespace&rsquo;s <code>vc:minVersion="1.1"</code>
      (<code>vc:maxVersion="1.0"</code> pins a 1.0 target);
      <code>leptris_xsd_version()</code> tells you which semantics a
      compiled schema carries. One deliberate boundary remains:
      <code>xs:anyAttribute</code> is lax by design — undeclared
      attributes are accepted, and strict-undeclared-element handling
      is owner-scoped. The conformance gate is a differential corpus
      against the libxml2 oracle (the Nokogiri-parity target&rsquo;s
      own engine): 1056 schema/instance pairs vendored from
      libxml2&rsquo;s <code>test/schemas</code>, with a pinned
      monotonic pass floor and a disposition ledger for every row
      still open.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="relaxng">RELAX NG</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`const char* rng =
    "<element name='server' xmlns='http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0'>"
    " <attribute name='port'>"
    "  <data type='integer'"
    "   datatypeLibrary='http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-datatypes'/>"
    " </attribute></element>";

LeptrisRelaxNG g = leptris_rng_parse(rng, strlen(rng), &st);
LeptrisDocument d = leptris_parse_string("<server/>", 9, &st);
if (leptris_rng_validate(g, d) == 0)
    printf("%s\\n", leptris_rng_error(g));
// => 1:10: error: element "server" missing required attribute "port"

leptris_document_free(d);
leptris_rng_free(g);`}
    />
    <p>
      Errors accumulate: <code>leptris_rng_error_report()</code> hands
      back the whole list as structured records, and
      <code>leptris_rng_error_count</code> /
      <code>_message</code> / <code>_line</code> / <code>_column</code>
      enumerate entries individually
      (<code>leptris_rng_error</code> keeps returning the first
      message). <code>leptris_rng_parse_file()</code> compiles a
      schema from a path, resolving <code>&lt;include
      href="..."&gt;</code> relative to the file&rsquo;s directory,
      following Jing&rsquo;s override rules.
    </p>

    <h2 id="schematron">Schematron</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`const char* sch =
    "<schema xmlns='http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron'>"
    " <pattern><rule context='server'>"
    "  <assert test='@port'>server needs a port</assert>"
    " </rule></pattern></schema>";

LeptrisSchematron sch_h = leptris_schematron_parse(sch, strlen(sch), &st);
LeptrisDocument d = leptris_parse_string("<server port='8080'/>", 21, &st);
int ok = leptris_schematron_valid(sch_h, d);   // 1 = every assert passed

/* the report form: leptris_schematron_validate() returns the SVRL
 * document; leptris_schematron_error() names the fired assert */
leptris_document_free(d);
leptris_schematron_free(sch_h);`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>leptris_schematron_parse_phase()</code> compiles with a
      selected phase and <code>leptris_schematron_parse_file()</code>
      compiles from a path; schema errors publish to the thread-global
      error channel (<code>leptris_last_error</code>).
    </p>

    <h2 id="dtd">DTD</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <leptris/dtd.h>

const char* dtd =
    "<!ELEMENT server EMPTY><!ATTLIST server port CDATA #REQUIRED>";
LeptrisDTD* dt = leptris_dtd_parse(dtd, strlen(dtd));

LeptrisDocument d = leptris_parse_string("<server port='8080'/>", 21, &st);
LeptrisDTDError err = {0};
int ok = leptris_dtd_validate(d, dt, &err);  // 1 valid, 0 invalid
// on failure err carries element name + position + message, e.g.
// "Element 'server' missing #REQUIRED attribute 'port'"
leptris_dtd_error_free(&err);
leptris_document_free(d);
leptris_dtd_free(dt);`}
    />
    <p>
      A document&rsquo;s own internal subset rides along via
      <code>leptris_document_get_dtd()</code>; external subsets parse
      with <code>leptris_dtd_parse_external_subset()</code>, and a
      parameter-entity loader hook
      (<code>leptris_dtd_set_pe_loader()</code>) lets the host control
      entity resolution. Shipped today: element content models (EMPTY/
      ANY/mixed/element), attribute defaults
      (<code>#REQUIRED</code>/<code>#IMPLIED</code>/<code>#FIXED</code>)
      and attribute-type validation. Parameter-entity expansion and
      ENTITY-typed attribute resolution are on the roadmap.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ruby">Ruby</h2>
    <p>
      The binding exposes all four families under
      <code>Leptris::XML</code> (snippets verified against the shipped
      gem):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "leptris"

# XSD — compile once, validate many
xsd = Leptris::XML::XSD.compile(<<~XSD)
  <xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
    <xs:element name="server">
      <xs:complexType>
        <xs:attribute name="port" type="xs:integer" use="required"/>
      </xs:complexType>
    </xs:element>
  </xs:schema>
XSD
doc = Leptris::XML.parse('<server port="http"/>')
xsd.valid?(doc)                # => false
xsd.validate_errors(doc)
# => ["element 'server' @1:22: attribute 'port': lexical value does not match its type"]

# RELAX NG
rng = Leptris::XML::RelaxNG.parse(<<~RNG)
  <element name="server" xmlns="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0">
    <attribute name="port">
      <data type="integer" datatypeLibrary="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-datatypes"/>
    </attribute>
  </element>
RNG
rng.valid?(Leptris::XML.parse('<server port="8080"/>'))   # => true
rng.validate_errors(Leptris::XML.parse('<server/>'))
# => [{line: 1, column: 10, message: 'element "server" missing required attribute "port"'}]

# Schematron
sch = Leptris::XML::Schematron.parse(<<~SCH)
  <schema xmlns="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron">
    <pattern><rule context="server">
      <assert test="@port">server needs a port</assert>
    </rule></pattern>
  </schema>
SCH
sch.valid?(Leptris::XML.parse('<server port="8080"/>'))   # => true

# DTD
dtd = Leptris::XML::DTD.parse("<!ELEMENT server EMPTY>")
dtd.valid?(Leptris::XML.parse('<server/>'))               # => true`}
    />
    <p>
      XSD additionally offers <code>compile_file</code> /
      <code>compile_set</code> (imports resolve across the set) and
      <code>builtin_valid?</code> for direct access to the built-in
      lexical table. A schema that cannot be used at all raises
      <code>Leptris::XML::Error</code>; a well-formed but invalid
      schema compiles to a handle whose <code>#error</code> carries
      the engine&rsquo;s detail.
    </p>

    <h2 id="python">Python</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="python"
      code={`import leptris
from leptris import XML

xsd = leptris.XSD('''<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <xs:element name="server"><xs:complexType>
    <xs:attribute name="port" type="xs:integer" use="required"/>
  </xs:complexType></xs:element>
</xs:schema>''')

doc = XML('<server port="http"/>')
xsd.validate(doc)        # => False
xsd.error_log            # => ["element 'server' @1:22: attribute 'port': "
                         #      "lexical value does not match its type"]

rng = leptris.RelaxNG('''<element name="server" xmlns="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0">
  <attribute name="port">
    <data type="integer" datatypeLibrary="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-datatypes"/>
  </attribute>
</element>''')
rng.validate(XML('<server port="8080"/>'))   # => True`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>error_log</code> is a property (the list from the most
      recent validate), and <code>leptris.DTD</code> mirrors the same
      shape (<code>validate</code> + <code>error_log</code>).
      <code>leptris.Schematron</code> follows the SVRL model instead:
      <code>validate()</code> returns the SVRL report document, and
      <code>is_valid</code> is the boolean.
    </p>

    <h2 id="surfaces">Surface coverage</h2>
    <p>
      Which surfaces carry which faces today:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="text"
      code={`                XSD    RelaxNG    Schematron    DTD
C              yes      yes         yes        yes
CLI            yes      yes         yes        yes
Ruby           yes      yes         yes        yes
Python         yes      yes         yes        yes
Rust           engine-side only (descriptor face; the public crate
               surface is DOM/XPath today)
TypeScript     not yet`}
    />
    <p>
      The C probe behind each column: <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD</code> for
      compile + validate (<code>LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD_SET</code> adds the
      multi-document set face), <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_RELAXNG</code>,
      <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_SCHEMATRON</code> and
      <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_DTD</code>. All four faces are part of the
      core engine build — no compile-time option turns them off — so
      every shipping build answers yes to all of them.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="note">
      Every snippet on this page executed against the shipped artifacts
      before publishing (engine v{VERSIONS.lib}, gem 1.9.418.1, wheel
      1.9.418.0). The conformance ledger behind the XSD and RelaxNG
      lanes is suite-driven — verdict waves from the field become
      vendored regression pins in the engine repository.
    </Callout>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
